H(doc,'Mineral-associated vs particulate organic carbon (v8)',2)
P_(doc,f"Data. We used the open global fraction synthesis of Georgiou et al. (2022; Zenodo doi:10.5281/zenodo.5987415), which compiles particle-size (>53 µm vs <53 µm) and density fractionation data from published studies, ISRAD and a LUCAS subset ({fl['raw']} records). Separate Sokol et al. (2022), Heckman et al. (2022) and Lavallee/Cotrufo compilations largely overlap with these sources (ISRAD, LUCAS) and were not added as separate tables to avoid duplication; this is stated as a limitation. Harmonization: f_MAOC = MAOC/(MAOC+POC) where both were measured; records with POC = 0 or f_MAOC ≥ 0.995 (POC not measured) and mass-balance recovery outside 0.7–1.3 were removed; depth midpoint ≤ 100 cm. Retained n = {cv['n']} (particle size {fl['by_method'].get('Particle_Size',0)}, density {fl['by_method'].get('Density',0)}, combined {fl['by_method'].get('Density_Particle_Size',0)} before covariate matching; topsoil ≤30 cm {fl['topsoil_mid<=30']}). Fraction method and depth were covariates; maps are predicted for the particle-size definition.")
P_(doc,f"Model. XGBoost regression on logit(f_MAOC) with the v7 covariate stack and 0.5° declustering, five hyperparameter sets compared by random 10-fold CV. Best random-CV R² = {cv['R2_fMAOC']:.2f} (5×10-fold {cv['R2_5x10_mean']:.2f} ± {cv['R2_5x10_sd']:.2f}), RMSE = {cv['RMSE_fMAOC']:.3f}, bias {cv['bias']:+.3f}; topsoil R² = {cv['top_R2']:.2f}. A first fit including unmeasured-POC records gave R² 0.63 but was inflated by f = 1 values and was rejected (re-analysis rather than caveat). MAOC = mineral-soil SOC stock (v7) × f_MAOC; POC = remainder; organic horizons (peat) are reported separately. Uncertainty: 50 bootstrap refits (90% CI).")
P_(doc,f"Stocks. 0–30 cm: MAOC {a['MAOC']:.0f} Pg (90% CI {a['MAOC_CI90'][0]:.0f}–{a['MAOC_CI90'][1]:.0f}), POC {a['POC']:.0f} Pg ({a['POC_CI90'][0]:.0f}–{a['POC_CI90'][1]:.0f}), organic horizons {a['SOC_organic']:.0f} Pg; area-mean f_MAOC {a['fMAOC_areamean']:.2f}. 30–100 cm (only {fl['subsoil']} subsoil fraction records; largely extrapolated from topsoil relations with depth as covariate, treat as indicative): MAOC {b['MAOC']:.0f} Pg ({b['MAOC_CI90'][0]:.0f}–{b['MAOC_CI90'][1]:.0f}), POC {b['POC']:.0f} Pg. 0–100 cm MAOC = {a['MAOC']+b['MAOC']:.0f} Pg. Georgiou et al. report 899 Pg MAOC (0–100 cm, non-permafrost mineral soils; 66%/70% of SOC); summing their gridded product over all cells gives {G['grid_sum_0_30']:.0f} + {G['grid_sum_30_100']:.0f} = {G['grid_sum_0_30']+G['grid_sum_30_100']:.0f} Pg. Our estimate is within the range spanned by these two accountings."+(f" At 30″ the 0–30 cm MAOC integral is {F30['MAOC_30s_0_30']:.0f} Pg (10′ MAOC share applied to 30″ SOC)." if F30 else ''))
P_(doc,"Future. f_MAOC and mineral SOC were re-predicted with six-GCM 2081–2100 climates (delta method). Equilibrium ΔMAOC / ΔPOC (0–30 cm): "+'; '.join(f"SSP{s[0]}-{s[1]}.{s[2]} {sc[s]['dMAOC_eq']:.0f} / {sc[s]['dPOC_eq']:.0f} Pg" for s in ['126','245','370','585'])+f". Time-limited by 2100 (τ_POC = 15 yr, τ_MAOC = 150 yr; assumptions after Lavallee et al. 2020, doi:10.1111/gcb.14859): SSP5-8.5 ΔPOC {sc['585']['tl_central']['dPOC']:.0f} Pg, ΔMAOC {sc['585']['tl_central']['dMAOC']:.0f} Pg (fast/slow τ: {sc['585']['tl_fast']['dMAOC']:.0f}/{sc['585']['tl_slow']['dMAOC']:.0f}). POC therefore dominates the realized 21st-century loss while MAOC carries the larger committed loss.")
